commit 0d39be5a3f129cf1f35e7812108a2184e2193315
Author:     Egor Pasko <pasko@chromium.org>
AuthorDate: Wed Jun 17 12:31:54 2026 -0700
Commit:     Copybara-Service <copybara-worker@google.com>
CommitDate: Wed Jun 17 12:34:46 2026 -0700

    android: Use futex for __cxa_guard_acquire
    
    Define _LIBCXXABI_USE_FUTEX on Android and Linux.
    
    Currently all initialization of static locals goes through a single
    global lock via __cxa_guard_acquire. So if an initializer of a single
    such variable takes time, all other initializers in the same process are
    waiting. The _LIBCXXABI_USE_FUTEX was implemented in 2019 and aimed at
    replacing the global lock with a per-object one:
    
    https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/commit/70ebeabfb8330f805a26dbb2f8af77fb0ab0c5f0
    
    I ran a couple of Pinpoint jobs on Android for these benchmarks:
    * startup.mobile
    * system_health.webview_startup
    
    There was only one 'improvement' of 0.3 milliseconds with p-value of
    0.037, which is (a) on the boundary of significance and (b) not
    perceptible even if we had 1000x more initialization contention of this
    type.
    
    A less obvious benefit of _LIBCXXABI_USE_FUTEX is not relying on
    pthread/libc. This can be useful for low level code in Chromium that in
    certain cases should avoid calling libc to avoid reentrancy. Examples
    are: PartitionAlloc and base::Lock. Unfortunately, _LIBCXXABI_USE_FUTEX
    is not supported on MacOS, so this utility is of limited use.
    
    I do not know how robust _LIBCXXABI_USE_FUTEX is, assuming it is high
    quality like most of LLVM.
    
    Bug: 524997700
    Change-Id: I887471239a8140769697357149253418af9b5a9f
    Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/7928017
    Reviewed-by: Nico Weber <thakis@chromium.org>
    Commit-Queue: Egor Pasko <pasko@chromium.org>
    Cr-Commit-Position: refs/heads/main@{#1648517}
