commit 1df2adafaa36e2bcff31e28cd814505382e04df6
Author:     Mark Mentovai <mark@chromium.org>
AuthorDate: Tue May 6 10:10:48 2025 -0700
Commit:     Copybara-Service <copybara-worker@google.com>
CommitDate: Tue May 6 10:13:10 2025 -0700

    Make libc++ a static_library instead of source_set on non-Windows
    
    https://crbug.com/40215211 documents a number of bad things that happen
    on macOS when the main executable’s size changes. As a result,
    https://crbug.com/40216333 (2022-02-26) froze the main executable’s size
    by introducing padding in the __TEXT segment. The size has been
    maintained ever since by adjusting the amount of padding as necessary.
    Because there’s almost no code in the main executable aside from a small
    stub to locate, load, and jump to the framework where the bulk of the
    application’s code lives, this has been manageable. Between 2022-02-26
    and 2025-04-11, the padding only needed to be updated 4 times on the
    main branch for non-DCHECK builds, a 285-day interval on average.
    
    Since 2025-04-11, we’ve seen an increase in the rate that the padding
    needs to change. It has happened 6 times since then, a 4-day interval on
    average. Many of these changes were just transitioning between two sizes
    for padding.
    
    The size can only accurately be determined to be correct or incorrect
    after Chrome undergoes signing, so each of these size changes results in
    a failure of the signing pipeline, which then requires an engineer to
    investigate, recalculate the padding, and make a code change. At a 4-day
    interval, this is unsustainable.
    
    My team determined that the sizes have been changing as a result of PGO,
    and in particular, PGO optimizing libc++. Depending on profile feedback,
    libc++ can be optimized differently, leading the main app executable’s
    size to fluctuate beyond the ±8kB window that’s permissible to avoid
    re-triggering any form of https://crbug.com/40215211.
    
    The main app doesn’t use much C++ at all. It only links two translation
    units, and the only use of libc++ I count between them is
    std::unique_ptr. Despite this, we noted that all of libc++ was
    being linked into the main executable.
    
    The presence of all of libc++ was attributable to its being defined as a
    source_set rather than a static_library. source_set semantics differ
    from static_library semantics in that object files (.o) from a
    static_library (.a) are only loaded by the linker and placed in its
    output when necessary to resolve a link-time dependency (unresolved
    symbol). By contrast, a source_set results in object files (.o) being
    presented directly to the linker, which will always load them and place
    them in its output, regardless of whether they’re needed to satisfy a
    dependency. That means that all of libc++ was present in the main app
    executable, when only a small fraction of it was actually necessary.
    Combined with the fact that libc++ was being PGO-optimized to very
    different sizes on successive builds, this was responsible for size
    fluctuations beyond the ±8kB safe window.
    
    To overcome this bug, libc++ and libc++abi are redefined as
    static_library rather than source_set, restoring expected semantics, and
    shrinking the size of the main executable by eliminating unnecessary C++
    library support code. This change is not being brought to Windows, where
    builds in the static_library configuration failed. The libc++abi change
    is implemented in a way that tracks the libc++ change, so that libc++abi
    remains a source_set that winds up appearing in full in libc++ when
    libc++ is a shared library (such as in the component=shared_library
    build), and is changed to a static_library when libc++ is also a
    static_library.
    
    The main app executable’s padding is recalculated in line with this
    change. The new padding size is 216kB, and because the code making it
    into the executable is now more tightly controlled and does not invoke
    any DCHECKs, the DCHECK distinction is no longer necessary.
    
    Bug: 415689582
    Change-Id: I2bd7dcc60e23122a5d49995043431139887b90a5
    Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/6512096
    Reviewed-by: Thomas Anderson <thomasanderson@chromium.org>
    Reviewed-by: Leonard Grey <lgrey@chromium.org>
    Commit-Queue: Mark Mentovai <mark@chromium.org>
    Cr-Commit-Position: refs/heads/main@{#1456441}
